---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const description = 'Explore Dither Yuki features, supported formats, dithering algorithms, current limitations, and the product roadmap.';

const capabilities = [
  ['Documents', 'multi-tab projects, Save As, recent files, crash recovery, and unsaved-change protection'],
  ['Import & Export', 'PNG, JPG, WEBP, BMP, TIFF, SVG, and ASCII export to TXT, ANSI, HTML, SVG, PNG, and JSON'],
  ['Layers', 'non-destructive effects, opacity, 12 blend modes, and undo/redo'],
  ['35+ algorithms', 'Bayer, Floyd–Steinberg, Atkinson, Sierra, halftone, stipple, ASCII, CRT, Glitch, Glow, Curves, Riemersma, and more'],
  ['Color Lab', 'palette import/export, color quantization, harmonies, ramps, and multiple palette-generation modes'],
  ['Patterns', 'a .dyuki pattern library and manager'],
  ['Interface', 'flexible workspace layout, keyboard shortcuts, and in-app updates'],
];

const roadmap = [
  'Video and animation support',
  'CMYK and improved color management',
  'More dithering algorithms and effects',
  'Expanded palette and pattern tools',
  'Performance improvements',
  'Better cross-platform support',
  'And much more',
];
---

<Layout
  variant="review"
  title="Dither Yuki Review — Features & Roadmap"
  description={description}
  canonical="/review/"
  image="/images/git-hub-cover.png?v=3"
  imageAlt="Dither Yuki — a colorful pixel-dithered dog portrait on a dark background"
>
  <div class="review-content">
    <section class="review-intro reveal" aria-labelledby="review-title">
      <h1 id="review-title"><strong>Dither Yuki</strong> is a beta-stage image dithering and palette studio built for experimenting with digital images, colors, and visual effects.</h1>
      <p>The workflow covers everything from opening an image and building a layer stack to working with palettes, applying dithering algorithms, and exporting the result.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="review-screenshot reveal reveal-media">
      <img
        src="/images/screnshot.png"
        alt="Dither Yuki desktop application with layer controls, image preview, dithering effects, and color palette tools"
        width="1317"
        height="870"
        fetchpriority="high"
        decoding="async"
      />
    </figure>

    <article class="review-copy">
      <section class="reveal" aria-labelledby="capabilities-title">
        <h2 id="capabilities-title">What it can do</h2>
        <ol>
          {capabilities.map(([name, detail]) => <li><strong>{name}</strong> — {detail}</li>)}
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="reveal" aria-labelledby="limitations-title">
        <h2 id="limitations-title">Current limitations</h2>
        <p>Dither Yuki is still in beta, so some features are intentionally outside its scope. It is not a painting, printing, batch-processing, or video tool, and there is currently no Linux version.</p>
        <p>Some algorithms also have implementation limitations. Error-diffusion methods currently run on CPU, while ASCII and Riemersma operate on the full document rather than progressive tiles.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="reveal" aria-labelledby="idea-title">
        <h2 id="idea-title">The idea</h2>
        <p>Dither Yuki is built around a simple idea: <strong>a dedicated space for experimenting with dithering, palettes, and digital image processing</strong> without turning the workflow into a complicated production suite.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="reveal" aria-labelledby="next-title">
        <h2 id="next-title">What's next</h2>
        <p>Dither Yuki is still evolving, and there is a lot more planned for the future.</p>
        <p>Right now, my main goal is to raise enough support for an Apple Developer Program membership. This will make it easier to distribute Dither Yuki on macOS and provide a smoother installation experience without unnecessary security warnings.</p>
        <p>After that, I want to keep improving the existing tools and expand what Dither Yuki can do.</p>
        <p>Planned features include:</p>
        <ul>
          {roadmap.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p>The goal is to keep growing Dither Yuki while preserving its main idea: a simple space to experiment, create, and play with images.</p>
      </section>
    </article>

    <img
      class="review-portrait reveal reveal-media"
      src="/images/dither-face-2.png"
      alt="Colorful dithered portrait artwork"
      width="477"
      height="615"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
  </div>
</Layout>
